const CONTEXT_NOTES = {
  "DOC-D4":["This collection of ten Russian-language documents comprises working materials produced by or for the Russian occupation administration in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territories. They are primary sources covering the ideological and educational frameworks being applied across occupied oblasts.","The collection includes approved reading and curriculum lists issued under the Russian State Children's Library and Ministry of Education frameworks; methodological manuals for the Year of the Family programme; competition guidance documents for higher education institutions operating under occupation administration; and materials on organising student activity within the Russian institutional framework.","Two documents are of particular analytical significance. The first is a document titled Ukrainian Crimes (2022–2023) — a compilation of alleged Ukrainian conduct intended for use in educational settings. The second is a manual on the Methods of TsIPSO (the Centre for Information and Psychological Operations) — Russia’s framing of Ukrainian information and psychological operations, distributed as a guide for occupation-era educators and administrators. These materials are relevant to Hub research on identity replacement, education capture, and the administrative architecture of annexation."],
  "DOC-E1":["This archive holds eleven issues of Лисичанський Вісник (the Lysychansk Herald), the Russian occupation administration’s local newspaper for Lysychansk in Russian-occupied Luhansk oblast, spanning from the inaugural edition (November 2023) through Issue 30.","The publication covers municipal services, local governance, cultural events, and commercial activity, alongside consistent framing of the occupation as liberation and Russian administration as a return to normality. Issue 1 includes reporting on Tatarstan produce supplied to Lysychansk supermarkets under the guardian-city relationship between Lysychansk and Tatarstan. LNR head Leonid Pasechnik features regularly in an administrative capacity.","The issues are relevant to Hub research on occupation media architecture, normalisation strategies, settler integration, and identity replacement. They are made available here as a primary source archive for cross-referencing with Hub analytical entries on governance and propaganda in Russian-occupied Luhansk oblast."],
  "DOC-E2":["This collection of ten photographs documents Russian mobilisation-related materials in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory, gathered across field visits between 2024 and 2026.","In Russian-occupied territories, conscription intersects with passportisation — the mass issuance of Russian Federation passports as a condition of access to services. Residents who have accepted Russian documentation acquire the obligations of Russian citizenship, including liability for military service. The photographs document the material outputs of this system: notices, administrative instruments, and related materials as encountered in the field.","The collection also includes advertising materials from Russian regions — including Yakutia — recruiting contract soldiers in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory. These advertisements are evidence of a secondary dynamic within Russian mobilisation: regional administrations competing to attract recruits from outside their own territory in order to register them against their regional contract quota before the federal centre imposes mandatory targets. The financial packages offered vary by region and reflect the degree of pressure individual administrations are under to meet their numbers. This creates an informal market in which Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory functions as a recruitment ground for competing Russian regional mobilisation programmes.","The collection is relevant to Hub research on passportisation, administrative entrapment, and the legal architecture of occupation. It should be read alongside analytical entries on those themes and the IDP survey data (DOC-C5/C7), which directly measures awareness of mobilisation-related risk behaviours among displaced persons from Russian-occupied territories."],
  "DOC-G1":["These reports were produced by the Ombudswoman of the self-proclaimed Donetsk People’s Republic (DNR). They are primary sources from within the occupation administration itself and should not be treated as reliable or independently verified accounts of socio-humanitarian conditions in Russian-occupied Donetsk: the Ombudswoman’s office operates within and for the occupation administration, and reports are shaped accordingly.","Their analytical value lies precisely in what they acknowledge — the complaints, grievances, and social problems the DNR apparatus considered it permissible to record and address. Patterns across the reports reveal the limits of tolerated discontent and the administration’s framing of social problems. Read against independent sources, they illuminate both the conditions of occupation and the mechanisms of occupation self-representation.","The collection is made available here as a primary source archive. Users should apply source-critical analysis and cross-reference findings with independently verified reporting on conditions in Russian-occupied Donetsk."],
  "DOC-G2":["These four reports were produced by the occupation city administration of Mariupol — the Администрация города (AG, City Administration) and, for the January–June 2024 period, the Администрация городского округа (AGO, City District Administration) — and are primary sources from within the occupation apparatus. They document the volume, submission channels, processing status, and thematic categories of civilian appeals across three reporting periods: January–July 2023, August–December 2023, and January–June 2024.","Read critically. The reports reflect how the occupation administration chose to categorise and represent civilian demand, not necessarily the underlying reality. The most analytically significant shift is between the first two reports: housing claims drop from 66% to 7% of total appeals between the Jan–Jul 2023 and Aug–Dec 2023 periods. This does not reflect resolved demand — it reflects a deliberate reclassification of housing replacement and compensation appeals into the social entitlements category, which rises from 14% to 42% in the same period. The Jan–Jun 2024 AG report makes this reclassification explicit: the social support category (48%) now incorporates replacement housing applications, legal clarification requests under Russian normative-legal acts (НПА), and formal disputes with commission decisions (несогласие с решением комиссии). Housing need has not disappeared — it has been reclassified as a welfare entitlement under Russian administrative law.","The Jan–Jun 2024 period also includes a parallel AGO report for the same city and months, with a substantially different submission channel profile: only 45% forwarded from higher bodies (versus 78% for the AG report), and significantly higher rates of direct in-person (29%) and email (19%) submission. The two parallel reporting structures reflect the layered, sometimes redundant administrative architecture Russia has imposed on occupied Mariupol; their combined H1 2024 figure of 5,748 appeals remains well below equivalent 2023 half-year totals. The anomalous AGO February figure (23 appeals versus 456 in January) is unexplained and warrants verification.","The series tracks a structural shift across the occupation period: from emergency reconstruction administration in the immediate post-siege phase (Jan–Jul 2023, housing 66%, 9,031 total appeals) to normalised bureaucratic dependency (Jan–Jun 2024, housing subsumed into social support, 4,524 appeals). The declining volume of appeals and the progressive reclassification of housing as a welfare matter mark the occupation’s transition from presenting itself as an active reconstruction actor to treating the consequences of destruction as routine administrative caseload."]
};

const DOCS = [
  {id:"DOC-A1",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209140",date:"March 2026",theme:["Economics","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Briefing",title:"Ghost Pensioners: Pension Fraud and Demographic Coercion in Mariupol",desc:"~134,600 ghost pensioners generating 1.68bn rubles/month in fraudulent payments — and a dual-use coercion tool against those who remain.",descriptor:"How Russia defrauds its own treasury while weaponising pension precarity against Mariupol's remaining elderly population.",summary:"Russia's occupation administration claims pension payments for approximately 134,600 individuals no longer present in Mariupol — generating an estimated 1.68 billion rubles per month in fraudulent federal budget expenditure via DNR banks outside standard treasury oversight. Documents the fraud mechanism and a second coercion function: the deliberate use of citizenship-stripping provisions to threaten remaining pensioners. Two accountability pathways: fraud against the Russian federal budget and potential IHL violations.",file:"Product5_GhostPensioners.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mNzsoisqwYJGZkxRRZG9jdkQwnk9P13I/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/ghost_pensioners.html",vizFile:"dashboards/ghost_pensioners_visual.html"},
  {id:"DOC-A2",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209003",date:"March 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Briefing",title:"Shadow Fleet and Sanctions Evasion at Mariupol Port",desc:"10–20 vessels/month, up to 160,000 tonnes. The customs post, the Temryuk re-registration route, and the dredging programme that expands capacity from Q3 2026.",descriptor:"Mapping the sanctions evasion architecture at Mariupol’s reoriented port: cargo flows, vessel counts, and the Temryuk re-registration route.",summary:"Since Autumn 2024, Mariupol port has been reoriented to bulk cargo at 10–20 vessels per month. The November 2025 customs post enabled first direct export clearances in January 2026. A parallel evasion route via Temryuk re-registers cargo to obscure Russian-occupied territory origin. Coal Harbor dredging (May–October 2026) will increase vessel capacity beyond the current 8,000-tonne ceiling.",file:"Product4_ShadowFleet_v2.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/16N1-iJTPah7ZPCv6wJWxYFX_Uz4Y06XH/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/shadow_fleet_briefing.html",vizFile:"dashboards/shadow_fleet_v2.html",vizLabel:"Shadow fleet: interactive visual",vizFile2:"dashboards/mariupol_port_network.html",vizLabel2:"Port network: interactive visual"},
  {id:"DOC-A3",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20208939",date:"March 2026",theme:"Governance",format:"Briefing",title:"Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant: Tactical Exploitation Under Occupation",desc:"Russia is licensing ZNPP reactors it has excluded from its own 25-year energy plan. Three exploitation tracks: grid extraction, cryptocurrency mining, US-fuel reactor diplomacy.",descriptor:"Russia is licensing ZNPP reactors it has excluded from its own energy plan — a deliberate paradox that signals extraction, not integration.",summary:"Russia has licensed two ZNPP reactors under Russian nuclear law while simultaneously excluding the plant from its 25-year national energy plan. Three exploitation tracks: grid extraction for Mariupol’s industrial base, a cryptocurrency mining project under Presidential Administration oversight, and US-Russia negotiations over four American-fuel reactors. All six reactors remain in cold shutdown. Two critical power supply disconnections in November 2025 and March 2026.",file:"Product6_ZNPP.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1BOSzAWKjgb2R9m6M-Su_PBv6oOduZoOP/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/znpp_briefing.html",vizFile:"dashboards/znpp.html"},
  {id:"DOC-A4",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209557",date:"March 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Briefing",title:"Grain Expropriation in Russian-occupied Ukraine: State Operators, Control Networks, and Beneficiary Structures",desc:"Over 4 million tonnes of Ukrainian grain stolen. Full anatomy of the State Grain Operator (DZO): personnel, finances, legal structures, and accountability.",descriptor:"The full organisational anatomy of Russia’s grain expropriation machine in Russian-occupied Zaporizhzhia — personnel, finances, legal structures, and accountability.",summary:"Russia has stolen over 4 million tonnes of Ukrainian grain (~$580 million) through the State Grain Operator (DZO) and parallel extraction networks. Documents DZO capitalisation, leadership, territorial operations, and a financial paradox: declared losses against a 9.8% harvest increase in 2024. Personnel identification, organisational hierarchy, and sanctioned individuals set out in full. The seizures constitute pillage under IHL.",file:"grain_publishable.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CrhGFYNcQh0mLE_ZM_cJiTekShMvimUx/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/grain_briefing.html",vizFile:"dashboards/dzo.html"},
  {id:"DOC-A10",date:"June 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Briefing",title:"The Cost of Occupation: Living Standards in Mariupol, April 2026",desc:"Cost of living rising faster than incomes. Grocery basket 80 to 120% more expensive than Rostov or Taganrog. Water tariffs up close to sevenfold. New property charges. Drawn from occupation tariff decisions and fieldwork price data.",descriptor:"The cost of living in Mariupol is rising faster than incomes - and it is already more expensive than the Russian regions the city is being absorbed into.",summary:"Assessment of the cost of living in occupied Mariupol as of April 2026. Even with projected real income of up to RUB 75,000, food costs have climbed several-fold — typical monthly grocery spend runs RUB 45,000 to 60,000 for two people. The basic basket costs 80 to 120% more than in Rostov or Taganrog. Water tariffs rise close to sevenfold between December 2025 and June 2026; a reinstated 330-litre daily norm inflates bills for unmetered households. New property-based charges and a monopolised services market compound the fiscal pressure. The pattern is consistent with the fiscal direction of the city's 2026 budget: extraction from the population rather than reconstruction for it. Co-authored with Petro Andriushchenko.",file:"mariupol-socioeconomic-april-2026.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/mariupol_living_standards.html",vizFile:"dashboards/mariupol_utility_tariffs.html",vizLabel:"Utility tariffs: interactive visual",vizFile2:"dashboards/prices_april_2026.html",vizLabel2:"Market prices: interactive visual"},
  {id:"DOC-A6",date:"June 2026",theme:["Economics","Governance"],format:"Briefing",title:"Beijing's Quiet Stake in Occupied Ukraine",desc:"China is becoming the indispensable enabler of Russia's occupation economy and the West holds a lever it has barely touched.",descriptor:"Russia is becoming the junior partner in its own occupation — how China's equipment, finance, and Belt and Road networks are locking in the strategic asymmetry.",summary:"Examines China's deepening commercial role in the Russian-occupied territories of Ukraine. At least seventeen Chinese companies now operate openly in the occupied regions; the economy is described as 'totally yuanised,' with Chinese currency in circulation at seventy-nine bank branches, Chinese payment systems routing trades via Telegram, and Chinese relay stations forming the telecommunications backbone. Traces a three-stage engagement logic: private equipment suppliers first, Belt and Road para-diplomatic networks second, and now Russian courtship of Chinese state capital — each step deliberately deniable. The strategic asymmetry favours Beijing: Russian operators pay an 8–15% premium for Chinese-routed goods because they have no alternative. Argues that the West holds enforcement levers it has barely touched — the firms involved operate openly in European markets — and that occupied Ukraine is a laboratory for sanctions-evasion techniques applicable to any future contest involving China.",file:"Op-Ed - Beijing's Quiet Stake in Occupied Ukraine.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/beijing_quiet_stake.html"},
  {id:"DOC-A9",date:"June 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Briefing",title:"Mariupol's 2026 Budget Amendment: Control and Extraction Over Reconstruction",desc:"Official deficit up 74%, own revenues covering only 22.5% of spending. Education tops half the budget (RUB 4.266bn). Housing and utilities cut by 43%. Drawn from the occupation administration's own published figures.",descriptor:"Russia is optimising the cost of occupation rather than scaling Mariupol's recovery - analysis of the May 2026 budget amendment using the occupation administration's own published figures.",summary:"Analysis of the amendment, dated 7 May 2026, to the budget of the Mariupol urban district for 2026 and the 2027-2028 planning period. Official deficit rises from RUB 375.6 million to RUB 654.9 million (up 74%), but own revenues cover only 22.5% of spending, leaving a real gap of RUB 5.785 billion closed by drawing down reserves. Federal transfers fall by RUB 1.535 billion while planned personal income tax rises 55.9%, indicating stronger fiscal extraction from residents. Education rises to RUB 4.266 billion (over 57% of total spending); housing and utilities cut by 43%. Culture, sports, and youth policy stay protected. The 2027-2028 plan signals contraction of roughly one-third. Co-authored with Petro Andriushchenko.",file:"mariupol-budget-amendment-2026.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/mariupol_budget_amendment.html",vizFile:"dashboards/mariupol_budget_dynamics.html"},
  {id:"DOC-B1",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20184756",date:"April 2026",theme:"Education and Militarisation",format:"Briefing",title:"Voin: The Architecture of Child Soldier Training in Russian-occupied Ukrainian Territories",desc:"Hub analytical note on Voin’s structure, programme areas, and presence in Mariupol and Russian-occupied Zaporizhzhia.",descriptor:"Inside Voin: how Russia’s federally funded child soldier programme operates across Russian-occupied Ukraine — and what it is building toward.",summary:"Analytical note documenting Voin (ВОІН), Russia’s most centralised and explicitly military youth programme. Exclusively federally funded, applied coercively via forced passportisation. In Mariupol, a Voin complex is under construction on a former Ukrainian children’s camp, with capacity for 300 children. Russian state projections: 200,000+ trained personnel under 25 by 2030. Cross-references 14 Hub documents.",file:"Voin_Analytical_Note_April2026.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/voin.html"},
  {id:"DOC-B2",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20185532",date:"April 2026",theme:"Education and Militarisation",format:"Briefing",title:"The Voin Generation: Russia’s Militarisation of Children in Russian-occupied Ukraine and the Coming Strategic Threat",desc:"600,000–650,000 children in a five-tier militarisation pipeline. The cohort reaches conscription age between 2028 and 2034.",descriptor:"Russia is building its next army from the children of Russian-occupied Ukraine — the pipeline, the numbers, and the strategic logic.",summary:"An estimated 600,000–650,000 children in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territories have undergone military training in some form, with youth enrolment in militarist organisations rising 26-fold since 2015. Maps a five-tier militarisation architecture from preschool indoctrination through Voin training centres. 45.8 billion rubles directed to youth militarisation in 2024 alone. The cohort reaches conscription age between 2028 and 2034.",file:"Voin_Generation_Youth_Militarisation.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/voin_generation.html"},
  {id:"DOC-B3",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20200344",date:"March 2026",theme:"Identity, Culture and Religion",format:"Briefing",title:"Controlling Faith by Force: Russia’s Systematic Religious Persecution in Russian-occupied Ukrainian Territories",desc:"800–900 communities closed or underground. 70 clergymen killed or tortured. 95–100% suppression across major denominations.",descriptor:"The systematic elimination of religious freedom in Russian-occupied Ukraine — documented denomination by denomination, mechanism by mechanism.",summary:"Between 2014 and 2025, Russia eliminated the plural religious landscape of Russian-occupied Ukraine. Documents five interlocking mechanisms of persecution and the structural role of the ROC as an instrument of state power. Argues the conduct meets Rome Statute thresholds for religious persecution. Primary sources: EHRG/ISRS 2025; Havrylov 2026.",file:"Religious_Persecution_CITED.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TDxAaLcS97fbicxg8VaCjGe84TGV5SmN/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/religious_persecution_briefing.html",vizFile:"dashboards/religious_persecution.html"},
  {id:"DOC-B6",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209309",date:"March 2026",theme:["Civilian Life","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Briefing",title:"Mariupol Property Seizure Programme: Evidence from Occupation Administrative Records",desc:"The occupation administration’s own registry data: 8,526 residential properties, 2,220 reclaimed, and the texture of commercial seizure.",descriptor:"Russia’s property seizure machine in Mariupol — documented through the occupation’s own administrative records, address by address.",summary:"Uses occupation administration’s own registry data to document systematic property classification and transfer under the Russian beskhozyanost (ownerlessness) mechanism. Residential programme: 8,526 properties, 90.7% ownerless, 2,220 reclaimed. Commercial programme: ~460 unique properties, including systematic targeting of Ukrainian chain brands.",file:"housing_mariupol_note.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1tmUzQlM8d3HjGMrQaRDdAwse9bm077dg/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/mariupol_housing.html",vizFile:"dashboards/mariupol_property.html"},
  {id:"DOC-B7",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20200587",date:"January 2026",theme:"Governance",format:"Briefing",title:"Documentation, Identity, and Return: A Policy Road Map for Ukraine’s Citizens in Russian-occupied Territory",desc:"Ukraine’s return framework is legally coherent but operationally inaccessible. Three structural failures, European precedents, and a workable fix.",descriptor:"Ukraine’s citizens in Russian-occupied territory face a legal system that exists on paper but not in practice — this road map shows how to fix it.",summary:"Identifies three structural failures in Ukraine’s return framework: institutional fragmentation, absence of consular identification infrastructure, and Russia’s deliberate documentary erasure. Drawing on European precedents (UK BN(O) scheme, COVID-era remote verification), recommends designating the MFA as lead body for remote identity verification and reconstituting a single coordinating institution for the return pipeline.",file:"road_map_policy_brief.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iCMK5Dvs9_Q70aUpUMmb9ff1yETHbG0l/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/road_map.html"},
  {id:"DOC-C2",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20210256",date:"March 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Dashboard",title:"Russia’s Guardianship Programme: Interactive Research Dashboard",desc:"City-twinning pairings, promised vs delivered commitments, corruption cases, and the analytical finding that corruption is the operating mode — not an exception.",descriptor:"Interactive tracker of Russia’s guardianship reconstruction programme — pairings, commitments, delivery gaps, and systemic corruption patterns.",summary:"Interactive dashboard documenting Russia’s city-twinning programme assigning Russian cities as ‘guardians’ of Russian-occupied Ukrainian counterparts. Covers pairings, promised versus delivered commitments, programme timeline, corruption cases, and analytical patterns. Key finding: corruption is the operating mode, not an exception.",file:"guardianship_programme_dashboard__1_.html",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/file/d/12wvsHbIseqQrhcI23KH9Ud5v3-5yKSce/view?usp=drive_link",dashboardFile:"dashboards/guardianship.html",datasetFile:"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1VCNxHHU8sqnYM3j5B0Lt-TtTlvUI26eP/preview"},
  {id:"DOC-C3",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20210256",date:"March 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Data",title:"Russia’s Guardianship Programme: Underlying Research Dataset",desc:"Six sheets: pairings, promised vs delivered, timeline, sources, corruption cases, analytical patterns.",descriptor:"The full research dataset behind the guardianship programme — six sheets covering pairings, commitments, corruption cases, and analytical patterns.",summary:"Six-sheet research dataset underpinning the guardianship programme dashboard (DOC-C2). Covers city-district pairings, promised vs delivered commitments, programme timeline, primary sources, individual corruption cases, and synthesised corruption analytical patterns with Hub dimension tagging.",file:"Copy_of_russia_guardianship_programme.xlsx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1VCNxHHU8sqnYM3j5B0Lt-TtTlvUI26eP/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true"},
  {id:"DOC-C4",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20282986",date:"April 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Data",title:"Mariupol Occupation Economy: Registered Entity Dataset",desc:"1,448 registered entities. 100% listed as active. 3.5% filed financial data. Source data for the occupation economy dashboard.",descriptor:"The raw registry — 1,448 entities registered in Russian-occupied Mariupol, with financial reporting data for 3.5% of them.",summary:"Raw dataset of 1,448 legal entities registered in Mariupol under Russian occupation administration, sourced from the Russian commercial registry. Includes OGRN numbers, legal names, status, addresses, and financial data fields. Only 3.5% filed any financial data. Primary source for DOC-C6.",file:"Mariupol_occupation_economy.xlsx",gdocs:"data/mariupol_economy.xlsx",dashboardFile:"https://docs.google.com/viewer?url=https%3A%2F%2Ftotinsights.github.io%2Ftot-insights%2Fdata%2Fmariupol_economy.xlsx&embedded=true"},
  {id:"DOC-C5",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20200390",date:"September 2025",theme:["Civilian Life","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Data",title:"IDP Attitudes Survey: Raw Dataset (n=1,001)",desc:"Full cleaned survey dataset. Variables: displacement status, institutional effectiveness ratings, contact with Russian-occupied territories, risk behaviours, resistance attitudes.",descriptor:"The underlying survey data — 1,001 IDPs on life under occupation, resistance, and what support they think is worth the risk.",summary:"Full cleaned dataset from a survey of 1,001 IDPs from Russian-occupied Ukrainian territories. Variables cover displacement status, IDP self-identification, demographics, institutional effectiveness ratings, contact with Russian-occupied territories, attitudes toward Russian rule, and assessments of non-violent resistance actions. Data in Ukrainian. Source for DOC-C7.",file:"TOT_IDP_Polling__C_.xlsx",gdocs:""},
  {id:"DOC-C6",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20308286",date:"April 2026",theme:"Economics",format:"Dashboard",title:"Mariupol Occupation Economy: Interactive Dashboard",desc:"Five charts. 73% ООО entities. Zero state/municipal entities filed financial data. Three top streets bear Soviet names Russia reversed from Ukraine’s 2015 decommunisation law.",descriptor:"Five charts on the structure of Mariupol’s occupation economy — and what deliberate financial opacity tells us about how annexation actually works.",summary:"Interactive five-chart dashboard visualising Russian-occupied Mariupol’s registered economy. Key findings: 73% ООО entities; only 3.5% filed financial data with zero state/municipal entities filing anything; three top streets bear Soviet names reversed from Ukraine’s 2015 decommunisation law.",file:"mariupol_occupation_economy_dashboard.html",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/file/d/10dGMtOpL2PejxnWBF9mnTIp-ysWyeZDz/view?usp=drive_link",dashboardFile:"dashboards/mariupol_economy.html",datasetFile:"https://docs.google.com/viewer?url=https%3A%2F%2Ftotinsights.github.io%2Ftot-insights%2Fdata%2Fmariupol_economy.xlsx&embedded=true"},
  {id:"DOC-C7",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20199360",date:"September 2025",theme:["Civilian Life","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Dashboard",title:"IDP Attitudes Survey: Interactive Dashboard",desc:"76.4% rate Ukrainian government ineffective. 61.6% believe hidden resistance exists. Top resistance actions: evacuation, medicine, essential goods.",descriptor:"What 1,001 IDPs think about life under Russian occupation, the Ukrainian government, and which resistance actions are worth the risk.",summary:"Interactive dashboard presenting findings from a survey of 1,001 IDPs from Russian-occupied territories. Key findings: 76.4% rate Ukrainian government ineffective vs 48.9% for international humanitarian organisations; 58.5% disagree that people who stayed are satisfied with Russian rule; 61.6% believe hidden resistance exists. Top resistance actions: evacuation (46.5%), medicine (42.8%), essential goods (41.5%).",file:"TOT_IDP_polling_dashboard_v2.html",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jvPCxMNXe83miXATWlJ0S_gHAsZDwXFm/view?usp=drive_link",dashboardFile:"dashboards/idp_polling.html"},
  {id:"DOC-D2",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209424",date:"December 2025",theme:"Accountability and Legal",format:"Briefing",title:"Russia’s Pipeline of Persecution: A Systematic Campaign of Crimes Against Humanity in Russian-occupied Ukraine",desc:"Seven interlocking crimes against humanity. Over 20,000 civilians estimated in Russian custody. 168 released in exchanges by September 2025.",descriptor:"Seven interlocking crimes, one system — the legal and humanitarian case for treating Russia’s persecution of Ukrainian civilians as crimes against humanity.",summary:"Argues that Russia’s treatment of Ukrainian civilians constitutes a pipeline of seven interlocking crimes against humanity under the Rome Statute. Scale: over 20,000 civilians estimated in Russian custody, 168 released by September 2025. Five categories of recommendation including a UK-initiated UN Contact Group. Co-authored with Scott Martin (international criminal law).",file:"CSNS_Russia_s_Pipeline_of_Persecution_Ukraine.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qLuUC9T94Az2L2535G_mXs5C5nVnHc3g/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/pipeline_persecution.html"},
  {id:"DOC-D3",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20209664",date:"March 2026",theme:["Identity, Culture and Religion","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Briefing",title:"Russia Is Ethnically Cleansing Mariupol",desc:"Pre-war population: 450,000. Remaining: ~100,000, of whom 70% are pensioners over 60. Russian settler inflows: ~2,200/month. Within twelve years, the pre-war Ukrainian population will fall below 5,000.",descriptor:"At current rates, Mariupol’s pre-war Ukrainian population will effectively cease to exist within twelve years. The numbers, the mechanisms, and the plan.",summary:"Data-driven analytical essay making the explicit case for ethnic cleansing in Mariupol. Combined attrition of 710 pre-war residents/month through death, departure, and collapsed birth rates. Russian settler inflows ~2,200/month, with Russians projected to outnumber pre-war residents before end of 2026. Documents the federal Zemsky resettlement infrastructure and ISW assessment of a ten-year repopulation plan.",file:"ethnic_cleansing_mariupol_ei.docx",gdocs:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1wGt-gItK42Huxg25cJrprPcCa34fBzqm/edit?usp=drive_link&ouid=111550246717918057564&rtpof=true&sd=true",briefingFile:"briefings/ethnic_cleansing.html"},
  {id:"DOC-D4",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20308490",date:"April 2026",theme:"Information Space",format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Occupation Education and Propaganda Manuals: Source Document Collection",desc:"Ten Russian-language documents including curriculum lists, Year of the Family methodology, a ‘Ukrainian Crimes’ document, and a TsIPSO methods guide.",descriptor:"Primary source collection — Russian occupation-era education manuals, approved reading lists, and propaganda methodology documents.",summary:"Ten Russian-language documents comprising working materials produced by or for the Russian occupation administration. Includes Ministry of Education curriculum lists, Year of the Family methodological manuals, a document titled ‘Ukrainian Crimes (2022–2023)’, and a manual on the Methods of TsIPSO. Relevant to research on identity replacement, education capture, and the administrative architecture of annexation.",file:"manuals.zip",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1lsl4Y_8BQkYMBl5f1hOs4dBfC-w5Hhyu",dashboardFile:"dashboards/manuals.html"},
  {id:"DOC-E1",date:"April 2026",theme:"Information Space",format:"Primary Sources",title:"Lysychansk Vestnik: Occupation Newspaper Archive (Issues 1–30, 2023–2026)",desc:"Eleven issues of the Russian occupation administration’s local newspaper for Lysychansk, Russian-occupied Luhansk oblast, from inaugural edition (November 2023) through Issue 30.",descriptor:"Primary source archive — eleven issues of Lysychansk’s occupation newspaper, documenting how Russia narrates its own rule to the people living under it.",summary:"Eleven issues of Лисичанський Вісник, the occupation administration’s local gazette for Lysychansk. Covers municipal services, governance, commercial activity, alongside consistent framing of the occupation as liberation. Includes reporting on Tatarstan produce under the guardian-city relationship and LNR leadership coverage. Relevant to research on occupation media architecture, normalisation, and settler integration.",file:"Lysychansk_Vestnik-.zip",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1EJet58Qp4ha4UL39M5GHkA1QLzwj3-1a",dashboardFile:"dashboards/lysychansk_press_archive.html"},
  {id:"DOC-E2",date:"March 2026",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Mobilisation Documentation: Field Photography Collection (2024–2026)",desc:"Ten photographs documenting mobilisation notices, administrative instruments, and regional recruitment advertising — including Yakutia contract recruitment — gathered 2024–2026.",descriptor:"Field documentation — photographic evidence of Russian mobilisation materials in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory, 2024–2026.",summary:"Ten photographs documenting Russian mobilisation-related materials in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory, gathered across field visits 2024–2026. Includes advertising materials from Russian regions (including Yakutia) competing to attract contract soldiers against regional quotas. Relevant to research on passportisation, administrative entrapment, and the legal architecture of occupation.",file:"mobilisation.zip",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12beQvY6ROZY1ue1AUMoXVPaJUF0OJAdv",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mobilisation/doc-e2.html"},
  {id:"DOC-F1",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20272693",date:"March 2026",theme:"Governance",format:"Briefing",title:"Sergey Kiriyenko and Russia’s Occupation of Ukrainian Territories: Architecture of Absorption",desc:"From Yeltsin’s technocrat to Putin’s proconsul. Kiriyenko’s mandate, cadre network, russification programme, and the fragilities in his occupation model.",descriptor:"The engineer of annexation — how Kiriyenko runs Russia’s political absorption of occupied Ukraine, and what the model means for policy.",summary:"Comprehensive analytical profile of Sergey Kiriyenko, First Deputy Chief of Staff and Kremlin’s de facto proconsul for occupied Ukraine since spring 2022. Documents the evolution of the Kremlin’s occupation management model (Surkov → Kozak → Kiriyenko), the referenda as manufactured mandate, the cadre pipeline via ‘Leaders of Russia’, regional patronage, russification mechanisms (passportisation, language erasure, youth militarisation), reconstruction as governance, and Kiriyenko’s 2025 remit expansion to other post-Soviet territories. Includes key judgements and policy implications.",file:"kiriyenko_brief.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/kiriyenko_brief.html",vizFile:"dashboards/kiriyenko.html"},
  {id:"DOC-F3",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20268167",date:"April 2026",theme:"Coercion, Resistance and Security",format:"Dashboard",title:"Violent Resistance Activity: Monitoring Dashboard (Dec 2024 – May 2026)",desc:"464 verified incidents across 36 biweekly periods. Occupied territories and Russian Federation. Escalating RF-territory tempo from Q4 2025.",descriptor:"464 verified violent resistance incidents tracked biweekly — occupied territory and Russian Federation — December 2024 to May 2026.",summary:"Interactive monitoring dashboard for verified violent resistance incidents in Russian-occupied Ukrainian territory and Russian Federation territory, December 2024 to May 2026. 464 total incidents across 36 biweekly periods. Five tabs: overview with stacked area chart; biweekly trend; occupied territory breakdown (246 incidents — vehicle attacks 26%, sabotage 35%); RF-territory breakdown (141 incidents — railway sabotage 33%); geography by oblast (Zaporizhzhia 26%, Kherson 24%). Documents escalation of RF-territory operations from Q4 2025.",file:"resistance_analytics_v2.jsx",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/resistance_analytics.html"},
  {id:"DOC-G1",date:"April 2026",theme:"Civilian Life",format:"Primary Sources",title:"DNR Ombudswoman: Social-Humanitarian Situation Reports (Primary Source Collection)",desc:"Five reports from the Ombudswoman of the self-proclaimed DNR. Read critically — their value lies in what the occupation administration acknowledged as social problems, not as independent accounts.",descriptor:"Primary sources from inside the occupation — DNR Ombudswoman reports revealing what grievances the administration considered permissible to record.",summary:"Collection of five socio-humanitarian situation reports produced by the Ombudswoman of the self-proclaimed Donetsk People’s Republic. These are primary sources from within the occupation administration and should not be treated as reliable independent accounts of conditions in Russian-occupied Donetsk. Their analytical value lies in what they acknowledge: the complaints and social problems the DNR apparatus considered acceptable to record and address. Patterns across the reports reveal the limits of tolerated discontent and illuminate occupation self-representation. Read against independent sources.",file:"Obzor-.zip",gdocs:"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1PzTTUjQwxqWMOj6w2otKbHNr3zftQv8Z",dashboardFile:"dashboards/obzor_dnr.html"},
  {id:"DOC-089",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20185365",date:"April 2026",theme:"Civilian Life",format:"Visual",title:"Departure and Return Trends, Occupied Zaporizhzhia Oblast, 2022–Spring 2025",desc:"Horizontal bar chart of recorded population displacement flows from occupied Zaporizhzhia Oblast: evacuation to Ukrainian-controlled territory (40%), EU migration (30%), departure to Russia (25%), return in spring 2025 (5%).",descriptor:"Return flows at 5%, Russia-bound departure exceeding EU migration — displacement data mapping the mechanics of entrapment in occupied Zaporizhzhia.",summary:"TOT Insights visual showing the distribution of recorded population displacement flows from occupied Zaporizhzhia Oblast across four destination categories. Evacuation to Ukrainian-controlled territory accounts for 40% of recorded movement; migration to the EU 30%; departure to Russia 25%; return in spring 2025 5%. The low return share is consistent with entrapment dynamics documented across occupied territories. Russia-bound departures exceeding EU migration suggests coerced or semi-coerced relocation as a significant displacement component. Derived analysis based on available movement data, April 2025.",file:"TOT Insights Visual, April 2025",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/zaporizhzhia_movement_trends.html"},
  {id:"DOC-H1",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20200217",date:"February 2026",theme:"Identity, Culture and Religion",format:"Briefing",title:"Russia’s Settler Colonial Programme in Occupied Ukrainian Territories",desc:"Zemsky relocation schemes, co-opted administrators, Rosatom placements, and organic migration. Over 80 named Russian civilian settlers identified. Budget doubled year-on-year.",descriptor:"Russia is running a structurally organised settler colonial programme across the four occupied oblasts and Crimea — documented through Zemsky budgets, co-opted administrators, and over 80 identified civilian settlers.",summary:"Analytical briefing applying settler colonial theory (Wolfe, Veracini) to Russia’s civilian relocation programme in occupied Ukrainian territories. Documents interlocking mechanisms: Zemsky relocation programmes (budget doubled year-on-year through at least 2027), civil administrator co-option, Rosatom and federal ministry placements, and organic migration of construction workers and ideologically motivated individuals. Over 80 named Russian civilian settlers identified through open-source data. Argues the programme constitutes a structural elimination of Ukrainian society through transfer, assimilation, and demographic displacement. Companion to DOC-H2.",file:"settler_colonialism_briefing.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/settler_colonialism.html"},
  {id:"DOC-H2",doi:"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20199696",date:"February 2026",theme:"Identity, Culture and Religion",format:"Briefing",title:"Who Moves to Occupied Ukraine? Settler Profiles from Russian Civilians",desc:"Catalogue of Russian civilian settlers drawn from Telegram, Russian media, VKontakte, and OSINT databases. Grouped by motivation: economic, ideological, family proximity, career, mixed.",descriptor:"Catalogue of Russian civilian settlers — first-person accounts and profiles grouped by motivation typology: economic, ideological, family proximity, career, and mixed.",summary:"Compilation of settler profiles based on publicly available sources (occupation administration Telegram channels, Russian media, VKontakte, RuTube, OSINT databases). Profiles use first names only. Grouped by primary motivation typology: Economic (Zemsky programme — 2 million rubles for five-year relocation, double the rural Russia rate; 2% mortgages in Mariupol); Ideological; Family proximity; Career/professional; Mixed. Motivation coding based on participants’ own statements where available. Companion to DOC-H1 analytical briefing on Russia’s settler colonial programme.",file:"settler_profiles_compilation.docx",gdocs:"",briefingFile:"briefings/settler_profiles.html"},
  {id:"DOC-083",date:"December 2025",theme:"Accountability and Legal",format:"Report",title:"A State-Engineered Architecture of Persecution: Russia’s Systematic Campaign Against Ukrainian Civilians in Occupied Territories",desc:"Analysis of Russian state-led repression of Ukrainian civilians since 2022 — surveillance, arbitrary detention, forced deportations, torture, enforced disappearances, and illegal imprisonment examined as a coordinated system constituting potential crimes against humanity.",descriptor:"Seven interlocking mechanisms of persecution, one coordinated state system — the legal and evidentiary case for treating Russia’s treatment of Ukrainian civilians as crimes against humanity.",summary:"Sole-authored report examining Russian state-led repression of Ukrainian civilians in occupied territories since 2022. Analyses surveillance, arbitrary detention, forced deportations, torture, enforced disappearances, and illegal imprisonment as components of a coordinated system rather than isolated incidents. Makes the legal case that the conduct constitutes potential crimes against humanity. Published by Forsvarets høgskole / CSNS, March 2026. Companion to DOC-D2 (co-authored with Scott Martin).",file:"Forsvarets høgskole / CSNS Report, 29 March 2026",gdocs:"https://csns.uk/2026/03/29/a-state-engineered-architecture-of-persecution-russias-systematic-campaign-against-ukrainian-civilians-in-occupied-territories/"},
  {id:"DOC-084",date:"March 2026",theme:["Education and Militarisation","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Journal Article",title:"Capturing the Minds: The Role of Child Deportation in Maintaining Russian Authority over Ukraine’s Occupied Territories",desc:"Academic analysis of Russia’s deportation of Ukrainian children as biopolitical control — coercing families, disrupting Ukrainian identity, and shaping future generations through filtration systems, adoption pathways, and ideological indoctrination.",descriptor:"Child deportation as a political instrument — how Russia uses the removal of children to discipline families, sever Ukrainian identity, and build future compliance across occupied territories.",summary:"Co-authored with Anastasia Romaniuk. Analyses Russia’s deportation of Ukrainian children from occupied territories as a form of biopolitical control. Examines how filtration systems, adoption pathways, and ideological indoctrination in Russian institutions coerce families, disrupt Ukrainian identity transmission, and project Russian authority across generations. Published in the European Journal of International Security (Cambridge University Press), March 2026.",file:"European Journal of International Security, 9 March 2026",gdocs:"https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/752354B4F5AB0983F4CE5A489FA10391/S205756372610056Xa.pdf/capturing-the-minds-the-role-of-child-deportation-in-maintaining-russian-authority-over-ukraines-occupied-territories.pdf"},
  {id:"DOC-085",date:"January 2026",theme:"Coercion, Resistance and Security",format:"Report",title:"Thresholds of Survival: The Resistance in Occupied Ukraine",desc:"Updated assessment of Ukrainian resistance in occupied territories, 2024–25: intensified surveillance has pushed activity underground, with a structural shift toward compartmentation, trust-based networks, and survivability over visibility.",descriptor:"How surveillance pressure is reshaping resistance in occupied Ukraine — from visibility to survivability, from broad networks to compartmented cells.",summary:"CSIS report documenting the evolution of Ukrainian resistance activity in occupied territories through 2024–25. Based on field research, interviews across free Ukraine, and open-source analysis. Finds that intensified Russian surveillance and control have pushed resistance further underground, with a shift toward compartmentation, trust-based networks, and survivability over visibility. Companion to the earlier CSIS report DOC-087.",file:"CSIS Report, 14 January 2026",gdocs:"https://www.csis.org/analysis/thresholds-survival-resistance-occupied-ukraine"},
  {id:"DOC-087",date:"June 2024",theme:"Coercion, Resistance and Security",format:"Report",title:"Crossing Thresholds: Ukrainian Resistance to Russian Occupation",desc:"Fieldwork-based report on Ukrainian resistance in de-occupied territories: its role in undermining Russia’s war effort, the realities faced by participants, personal testimonies, and recommendations for Ukraine and Western supporters.",descriptor:"Fieldwork-based portrait of resistance in de-occupied Ukraine — what it achieved, what it cost, and what Western policy should do about it.",summary:"CSIS report presenting fieldwork and interview-based research on Ukrainian resistance activity in territories subsequently de-occupied. Examines the operational role of resistance in undermining Russia’s war effort, documents the personal realities faced by participants, and draws on testimony to assess the costs and achievements of resistance under occupation. Includes policy recommendations for Ukraine and Western supporters. Preceded by the 2026 update DOC-085.",file:"CSIS Report, 18 June 2024",gdocs:"https://www.csis.org/analysis/crossing-thresholds-ukrainian-resistance-russian-occupation"},
  {id:"DOC-088",date:"May 2024",theme:"Information Space",format:"Journal Article",title:"Russian Propaganda Tactics in Ukraine’s Newly Occupied Territories",desc:"Evolution of Russian propaganda in occupied Ukraine after 2022: from Crimea-style information control to a two-tier messaging system for Russian and occupied Ukrainian audiences, relying on fear-based narratives.",descriptor:"How Russian propaganda adapted to newly occupied Ukrainian territory — from Crimea templates to fear-based dual-audience messaging, and what counter-strategies are available.",summary:"Article in Russian Analytical Digest (Issue 313) analysing the evolution of Russian propaganda tactics in Ukrainian territories occupied after 2022. Argues that Russia moved from the Crimea-style information control model to a two-tier messaging system targeting Russian and occupied Ukrainian audiences differently, with a heavier reliance on fear-based narratives and threat construction. Includes analysis of potential counter-strategies. Published May 2024.",file:"Russian Analytical Digest 313, 21 May 2024",gdocs:"https://www.ssoar.info/ssoar/bitstream/handle/document/101373/ssoar-russanald-2024-313-mcglynn-Russian_Propaganda_Tactics_in_Ukraines.pdf?sequence=1"},
  {id:"DOC-G2",date:"May 2026",theme:"Civilian Life",format:"Primary Sources",title:"Mariupol City Administration: Citizen Appeals Reports (Jan 2023 – Jun 2024)",desc:"Four infographic-format reports on civilian appeals to the occupation city administration — 9,031 appeals (Jan–Jul 2023) falling to 4,524 (Jan–Jun 2024). Housing dominates at 66% in early 2023, drops to 7% by late 2023: a reclassification shift, not resolved demand.",descriptor:"Primary sources from the occupation administration — quarterly civilian appeals data revealing housing reclassification, bureaucratic dependency, and the shift from emergency reconstruction to normalised occupation governance.",summary:"Four official reports from the Mariupol occupation city administration (AG) and city district administration (AGO) covering citizen appeals from January 2023 through June 2024. Total appeals decline from 9,031 (Jan–Jul 2023) to 4,524 (Jan–Jun 2024). Housing dominates at 66% in the post-siege period, drops to 7% by Aug–Dec 2023 — a reporting reclassification rather than resolved demand.",file:"otchyot.zip",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/otchyet_mariupol.html"},
  {id:"DOC-K1",date:"April 2026",theme:["Economics","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Visual",title:"Sanctions in Plain Sight: Western Road Machinery in Occupied Zaporizhzhia",desc:"Two visualisations: three pieces of sanctioned EU/NATO machinery (Vögele Super 1800-3, Wirtgen W200, Dynapac CP2700) identified on site, and the spare parts evasion network — China (XCMG, SANY, LiuGong), Turkey grey market, Kazakhstan parallel import — keeping them operational.",descriptor:"Three pieces of sanctioned Western road machinery identified on the Azov military logistics corridor — and the spare parts evasion network keeping them running.",summary:"Two analytical visualisations from the 2026 TOT Insights OSINT/HUMINT investigation into JSC VAD road construction in occupied Zaporizhzhia. Viz 1: machinery identification — three pieces of sanctioned EU/NATO-manufactured road construction equipment documented in field photographs on the Pryazovske–Shevchenko–Novokostiantynivka section: Vögele Super 1800-3 asphalt paver (Wirtgen Group, Germany), Wirtgen W200 milling machine (Wirtgen Group), and Dynapac CP2700 roller (Fayat Group, Sweden/France). All three parent groups are subject to EU, UK, and Ukrainian sanctions. Maintenance schedules and sanctions pressure points documented for each machine. Contractor: JSC VAD (AO VAD), sanctioned by EU, UK, and Ukraine; licence plates confirm deployment from St Petersburg. Viz 2: spare parts evasion network — China (XCMG, SANY, LiuGong dealer network with ₽3bn+ Russian warehousing), Turkey (grey market conduit for genuine Western components), and Kazakhstan (parallel import channel) as three documented bypass routes converging on JSC VAD operations on the Azov military logistics corridor. Road construction assessed as military infrastructure, not commercial.",file:"OSINT/HUMINT report on road construction in occupied Zaporizhzhia, TOT Insights Hub, 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/china_road_visualisations.html"},
  {id:"DOC-J1",date:"April 2026",theme:["Economics","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Visual",title:"Looting Donbas: The Mariupol Coal Export Scheme — George Oil Visualisations",desc:"Three visualisations: the four-day George Oil deal sequence (signing 19 March 2026, ₽441m dredging tender, George Oil Plus registration in Batumi), Mariupol port transformation into a bulk raw-material export terminal, and the sanctions circumvention scheme routing looted coal through a Georgian intermediary.",descriptor:"The George Oil agreement, Mariupol port conversion, and a sanctions circumvention scheme for exporting looted Donbas coal — three analytical visualisations from the TOT Insights investigation.",summary:"Three analytical visualisations from the 2026 TOT Insights investigation into the George Oil agreement and Mariupol port coal export scheme. Viz 1: Event sequence — the four-day coincidence around the 'DNR' government–George Oil signing (19 March 2026): a ₽441 million dredging tender for Mariupol's Coal Harbor published within four days, and George Oil Plus registered in Batumi within five days, indicating coordinated preparation rather than spontaneous commercial activity. Viz 2: Mariupol port transformation — five interlocking infrastructure elements converting the commercial sea port into a dedicated bulk raw-material export terminal, purpose-built for permanent operations. Viz 3: The export scheme — looted coal (and prospectively grain, sunflower, and salt) channelled through George Oil as a Georgian private intermediary, creating legal distance between occupation extractors and end markets, following the pattern documented across multiple occupied-territory resource extraction schemes. Companion to DOC-A2 (Shadow Fleet and Sanctions Evasion at Mariupol Port).",file:"ANALYTICAL_BRIEF_G, TOT Insights Hub, 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/george_oil_visualisations.html"},
  {id:"DOC-I1",date:"April 2026",theme:["Economics","Governance"],format:"Visual",title:"Chinese Belt and Road Expansion into Occupied Ukraine: Network Analysis Visualisations",desc:"Three visualisations: BRI paradiplomacy architecture (ANSO → Mariupol State University, March 2026), engagement timeline 2018–2026, and the Karansky quarry supply chain from Chinese equipment manufacturers to Russian military road construction.",descriptor:"China's Belt and Road paradiplomatic networks reach occupied Ukrainian territory for the first time — architecture, timeline, and the Karansky quarry supply chain connecting Chinese manufacturers to Russian military logistics.",summary:"Three analytical visualisations from the January–March 2026 TOT Insights OSINT and HUMINT investigation into Chinese Belt and Road network expansion into occupied Ukrainian territories. Viz 1: BRI paradiplomacy architecture — ANSO (Belt and Road science network core, founded at the initiative of the Chinese Academy of Sciences) as institutional hub, branching through the Silk Road University Alliance (72 members including Russian universities), BRI Enterprise Alliance, and Silk Road Dialogue Committee, reaching Mariupol State University in March 2026 — the first confirmed BRI network entry into occupied Ukrainian territory. Viz 2: Engagement timeline 2018–2026, tracing the stepping-stone logic from ANSO founding through academic and women's networks to occupied territory. Viz 3: Karansky quarry supply chain — Chinese equipment manufacturers Amma Construction and Zhongxin Heavy Industry supply crushing and mineral powder processing machinery that enables LLC Karansky Quarry (registered July 2022 in occupied Donetsk) to feed LLC Mostovik's road construction projects on the R-280 Novorossiya highway and Azov military logistics corridor.",file:"AR_-_CHBr analytical study, TOT Insights Hub, 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/bri_visualisations.html"},
  {id:"DOC-095",date:"April 2026",theme:["Economics","Civilian Life"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Official Market Valuation of Residential Property in Mariupol, 2026",desc:"Decree setting the official average market price per square metre of residential property in Russian-occupied Mariupol for 2026: 146,212 rubles on the primary market and 80,695 rubles on the secondary market.",descriptor:"Russian federal property valuation norms applied to Mariupol's housing market, with the secondary-market rate mandated for municipal services calculations: primary evidence of administrative integration.",summary:"Decree setting the official average market price per square metre of residential property in Russian-occupied Mariupol for 2026: 146,212 rubles on the primary market and 80,695 rubles on the secondary market. The secondary rate is designated for use in municipal services calculations. Documents the full integration of Mariupol's housing market into the Russian ruble-denominated system.",file:"Decree No. 721, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 6 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-095.html"},
  {id:"DOC-096",date:"April 2026",theme:["Governance","Economics"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Auction Decree: Municipal Property Lease, Lenin Prospekt & Metallurgov Prospekt, Mariupol",desc:"Decree authorising the electronic auction of two non-residential municipal properties in Mariupol on five-year leases under Russian federal competition law. Starting prices 1.05 and 1.89 million rubles per year.",descriptor:"Russian federal auction procedures applied to formerly Ukrainian city assets: two Mariupol commercial properties tendered via the RTS-Tender platform.",summary:"Decree authorising the electronic auction of two non-residential municipal properties in Mariupol, Lenin Prospekt 153 (74.6 sq m) and Metallurgov Prospekt 235 (165.8 sq m), for five-year lease contracts under Russian federal competition law. Auction conducted by LLC \"Kalmius\" (INN 9311026463) via the RTS-Tender platform. Starting prices 1.05 and 1.89 million rubles per year respectively. Documents the application of Russian municipal property law to formerly Ukrainian city assets.",file:"Decree No. 722, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 6 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-096.html"},
  {id:"DOC-097",date:"April 2026",theme:["Governance","Economics"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Subsidy Amendment: MUP Mariupolzhilkompleks Wage Arrears, January–February 2026",desc:"Decree amending a subsidy scheme to cover wage debt owed to employees of the municipal housing utility enterprise MUP \"Mariupolzhilkompleks\" for January–February 2026.",descriptor:"Occupation administration management of municipal wage arrears through Russian federal budget mechanisms: the housing utility enterprise as a dependent node in the occupation fiscal structure.",summary:"Decree amending a subsidy scheme to cover wage debt owed to employees of the municipal housing utility enterprise MUP \"Mariupolzhilkompleks\" for January–February 2026. Documents the occupation administration's management of municipal utilities and social obligations through Russian federal budget mechanisms.",file:"Decree No. 728, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 7 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-097.html"},
  {id:"DOC-098",date:"April 2026",theme:["Governance","Economics"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Auction Decree: Municipal Property Lease, Stroiteley Prospekt & Lenin Prospekt, Mariupol",desc:"Decree authorising the electronic auction of two further non-residential municipal properties in Mariupol on five-year leases under Russian federal law. Starting prices 2.07 and 1.32 million rubles per year.",descriptor:"A second batch of Mariupol municipal properties auctioned under Russian federal law: the continuing transfer of Ukrainian city assets into the Russian administrative and legal framework.",summary:"Decree authorising the electronic auction of two further non-residential municipal properties, Stroiteley Prospekt 39 (181.9 sq m) and Lenin Prospekt 151 (93.2 sq m), on five-year leases under Russian federal law. Starting prices 2.07 and 1.32 million rubles per year. Auction organiser LLC \"Kalmius.\" Documents the continuing transfer of Ukrainian municipal property into the Russian administrative and legal framework.",file:"Decree No. 746, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 9 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-098.html"},
  {id:"DOC-099",date:"April 2026",theme:["Education and Militarisation","Identity, Culture and Religion"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"“Development of Education in Mariupol” Municipal Programme, 2026–2028",desc:"Full text of the occupation administration's three-year education programme for Mariupol. Total budget 8.76 billion rubles. Objectives explicitly include \"patriotic upbringing\" and \"formation of traditional Russian spiritual-moral values.\"",descriptor:"The Russification of Mariupol's education system as a state programme: 8.76 billion rubles, measurable targets, and multi-year federal funding for \"patriotic upbringing\" and \"traditional Russian spiritual-moral values.\"",summary:"Full text of the occupation administration's three-year education programme for Mariupol. Total budget 8.76 billion rubles, funded jointly by the DNR republican budget, Russian federal budget, and Mariupol city budget. Programme objectives explicitly include \"patriotic upbringing\" and \"formation of traditional Russian spiritual-moral values\" alongside access to education. Targets full integration into Russian federal curriculum standards (FGOS). Documents the systematic Russification of Mariupol's education system as a state programme with measurable targets and multi-year federal funding.",file:"Decree No. 757, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 10 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-099.html"},
  {id:"DOC-100",date:"April 2026",theme:["Governance","Economics"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Full Auction Documentation: Municipal Property Leases, Mariupol (April–May 2026)",desc:"Complete auction documentation package for the electronic tender of two Mariupol municipal properties via the Russian federal RTS-Tender platform. Application window 15 April–5 May 2026; auction date 7 May 2026.",descriptor:"Complete Russian federal auction documentation for Mariupol municipal properties: full procedural integration into Russian federal procurement infrastructure, referenced against Russian federal law and DNR municipal regulations.",summary:"Complete auction documentation package for the electronic tender of two Mariupol municipal properties. Conducted via the Russian federal RTS-Tender platform under FAS Order No. 147/23. Application window 15 April–5 May 2026; auction date 7 May 2026. Documentation references Russian Civil Code, Russian federal competition law, and DNR municipal regulations. Demonstrates full procedural integration into Russian federal procurement infrastructure.",file:"Decree No. 766, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 14 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-100.html"},
  {id:"DOC-101",date:"April 2026",theme:["Civilian Life","Economics"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Official Sports Facility Tariffs: Municipal Sports Complex “Lider,” Mariupol, 2026",desc:"Decree approving ruble-denominated tariffs for paid services at the occupation administration's municipal sports complex across two former Mariupol stadiums. Rates range from 2,500 rubles per hour for full-size football pitches to 150 rubles per hour for running track use.",descriptor:"Ruble-denominated tariffs for a Mariupol sports complex operating across two former stadiums: routine occupation administration of civilian leisure infrastructure.",summary:"Decree approving ruble-denominated tariffs for paid services at the occupation administration's municipal sports complex operating across two former Mariupol stadiums (“Olimp” and “Zapadny”). Rates range from 2,500 rubles per hour for full-size football pitches to 150 rubles per hour for running track use. Documents the functioning of Russian-administered leisure infrastructure in occupied Mariupol.",file:"Decree No. 768, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 14 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-101.html"},
  {id:"DOC-102",date:"April 2026",theme:["Civilian Life","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Housing Repair Programme Amendment: War-Damaged Residential Properties, Mariupol",desc:"Decree amending a co-financed municipal programme for capital repair of residential apartments damaged in what the occupation administration terms \"military actions.\" Lists 21 specific apartment addresses receiving state-subsidised repair funded partly by residents.",descriptor:"Residents required to co-finance repair of damage caused by Russia's own military: the occupation administration frames war damage as a routine municipal housing maintenance matter.",summary:"Decree amending a co-financed municipal programme for capital repair of residential apartments damaged in what the occupation administration terms \"military actions.\" Lists 21 specific apartment addresses across Mariupol streets receiving state-subsidised repair funded partly by residents. Documents the occupation administration framing war damage as a routine municipal housing maintenance matter, with residents required to co-finance repair of damage caused by Russia's own military.",file:"Decree No. 770, Administration of the City District of Mariupol, DNR, 15 April 2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mariupol-admin-2026/doc-102.html"},
  {id:"DOC-103",date:"March 2020",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Donetsk People's Republic: Constitution (Consolidated Text, March 2020)",desc:"Consolidated text of the DNR Constitution adopted in 2014 and revised through March 2020. The constitutional foundation for the entire DNR legal order, including its legislative, executive, and judicial structures.",descriptor:"The constitutional basis of the DNR legal order: the document that underwrites every legislative act issued by the People's Council.",summary:"The consolidated text of the Constitution of the Donetsk People's Republic, as amended through March 2020. The Constitution establishes the DNR as a sovereign state with a presidential-parliamentary system, a bicameral legislature (the People's Council), and a Constitutional Court. It sets out fundamental rights, territorial provisions, and the competencies of state bodies. Analytically, the Constitution is the foundational document for the entire DNR legal order: every law cited in this collection (DOC-104 through DOC-108) was adopted under its authority and every decree (DOC-108) was issued within the executive powers it confers. The Constitution was modelled closely on the Russian Federation Constitution of 1993 and Belarus, reflecting Russian drafting influence from the outset.",file:"DNR Constitution, consolidated text, March 2020",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-103.html"},
  {id:"DOC-104",date:"December 2021",theme:["Governance","Education and Militarisation"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Donetsk People's Republic: Law on Education (Consolidated Text, December 2021)",desc:"Consolidated text of the DNR Law on Education as of December 2021. Modelled on the 2012 Russian federal education law, it governs the DNR school system, curriculum standards, and language of instruction.",descriptor:"The DNR education law that replaced the Ukrainian curriculum with Russian federal standards: the legal basis for the Russification of schooling in occupied Donetsk.",summary:"Consolidated text of the Law of the Donetsk People's Republic on Education, as amended through December 2021. The law is modelled on Russian Federal Law No. 273-FZ of 29 December 2012 and governs all levels of education from preschool through higher education within DNR-controlled territory. Key provisions govern the language of instruction (Russian as the state language), curriculum standards (based on Russian federal state educational standards, FGOS), and the licensing and accreditation of educational organisations. Read alongside the Mariupol education programme (DOC-099) and the ZO Ministry of Education regulations (DOC-116), the law documents the systematic replacement of the Ukrainian education system with Russian federal structures across occupied Donetsk. Cross-reference: RF Federal Law No. 423-FZ on military training in schools (DOC-121).",file:"DNR Law on Education, consolidated text, December 2021",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-104.html"},
  {id:"DOC-105",date:"March 2020",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Donetsk People's Republic: Law on Military Duty and Military Service (Law No. 08-INs, Consolidated March 2020)",desc:"Consolidated text of DNR Law No. 08-INs governing military duty, conscription, and military service obligations. The foundational statute for the DNR conscription system, amended by DOC-107 and activated by DOC-108.",descriptor:"The DNR conscription law: the statute that defines who must serve, on what grounds they can be exempted, and how the mobilisation machinery operates.",summary:"Consolidated text of Law No. 08-INs of the Donetsk People's Republic on Military Duty and Military Service, as of March 2020. The law defines the categories of persons subject to military duty, the procedure for conscription call-ups, grounds for deferral and exemption (including the provisions of Article 23, subsequently amended by DOC-107), and the obligations of citizens, employers, and state bodies during mobilisation. The statute is the foundational legal instrument for the DNR's conscription system and the statutory basis for the General Mobilisation Decree of February 2022 (DOC-108). Cross-reference: DNR Mobilisation Preparation Law (DOC-106); DNR Law No. 376-IINs (DOC-107); DNR General Mobilisation Decree No. 29 (DOC-108).",file:"DNR Law No. 08-INs on Military Duty and Military Service, consolidated March 2020",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-105.html"},
  {id:"DOC-106",date:"September 2021",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Donetsk People's Republic: Law on Mobilisation Preparation and Mobilisation (Law No. 10-INs, 22 September 2021)",desc:"DNR Law No. 10-INs governing the full cycle of mobilisation preparation, including the obligations of government bodies, enterprises, and citizens to support military readiness. The direct statutory basis for the DNR General Mobilisation Decree of February 2022.",descriptor:"The DNR mobilisation statute: the law that defines how the republic prepares for and conducts mass military mobilisation, activated by Decree No. 29 five months after its adoption.",summary:"Law No. 10-INs of the Donetsk People's Republic on Mobilisation Preparation and Mobilisation, adopted 22 September 2021. The law governs the full cycle of mobilisation planning and execution: the obligations of republican government bodies, municipal authorities, enterprises, and citizens in preparing for and conducting mobilisation; the powers of the Head of the Republic to declare mobilisation; and the responsibilities of the Ministry of Defence and other power structures in implementing mobilisation plans. The law was adopted five months before the General Mobilisation Decree (DOC-108) of 19 February 2022, indicating deliberate statutory preparation for the imminent invasion. The law includes a provision requiring educational institutions to contribute to pre-military training, which connects to the RF Federal Law on military training in schools (DOC-121).",file:"DNR Law No. 10-INs on Mobilisation Preparation and Mobilisation, 22 September 2021",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-106.html"},
  {id:"DOC-107",date:"Pre-February 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Donetsk People's Republic: Law No. 376-IINs — Amendment to Article 23, Law on Military Duty",desc:"DNR Law No. 376-IINs amends Article 23 of Law No. 08-INs (the conscription statute) to narrow the grounds on which citizens are exempt from call-up. Part of the pre-invasion tightening of the DNR military duty framework.",descriptor:"The DNR amendment that quietly narrowed conscription exemptions before the February 2022 mobilisation: stepwise legal preparation for mass call-up.",summary:"Law No. 376-IINs of the Second People's Council of the Donetsk People's Republic, amending Article 23 of Law No. 08-INs (DOC-105). Article 23 governs the grounds on which DNR citizens are not subject to conscription, including health-based exemption, criminal custody, ongoing investigation, and other categories. The amendment narrows these grounds, reducing the scope for lawful non-participation in military service. The timing, in the period before the General Mobilisation Decree of 19 February 2022 (DOC-108), identifies this as part of a deliberate stepwise preparation of the conscription legal architecture. The parallel LNR amendment (DOC-117) follows the same pattern.",file:"DNR Law No. 376-IINs, amendment to Article 23, Law on Military Duty, Second People's Council period",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-107.html"},
  {id:"DOC-108",date:"February 2022",theme:["Governance","Education and Militarisation","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Decree of the Head of the Donetsk People's Republic No. 29 — On the Conduct of General Mobilisation (19 February 2022)",desc:"DNR Decree No. 29, signed by Denis Pushilin on 19 February 2022 (five days before Russia's full-scale invasion), declaring general mobilisation across DNR territory and ordering all conscription-age men to report to military commissariats.",descriptor:"The DNR general mobilisation decree: signed five days before the invasion, activating the full conscription apparatus and ordering every military-age man to report.",summary:"Decree No. 29 of the Head of the Donetsk People's Republic Denis Pushilin, signed 19 February 2022, declares general mobilisation across all DNR-controlled territory. The decree cites increased Ukrainian military aggression as justification and directs the Ministry of Defence, Ministry of Internal Affairs, government bodies, enterprises, and citizens to fulfil their obligations under DNR mobilisation legislation (DOC-106). The timing, five days before Russia's full-scale invasion on 24 February 2022, positions this decree as a coordinated pre-invasion preparation. The decree was issued simultaneously with a parallel LNR general mobilisation order. The DNR People's Militia was subsequently absorbed into the Russian Armed Forces Southern Military District grouping. This document appears in two source archives; a single catalogue entry is maintained here.",file:"DNR Decree No. 29, 19 February 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/dnr-legal-acts/doc-108.html"},
  {id:"DOC-109",date:"May 2022",theme:["Governance","Identity, Culture and Religion"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation Presidential Decree No. 304 (25 May 2022) — Extension of Simplified Citizenship to Kherson Oblast and Occupied Zaporizhzhia",desc:"Decree No. 304 extends Russia's simplified citizenship naturalisation procedure to residents of Kherson Oblast and occupied parts of Zaporizhzhia, three months after Russian military seizure and five months before formal annexation.",descriptor:"Step 5 of 10 in the passportisation sequence: simplified citizenship extended to Kherson and Zaporizhzhia residents before the annexation that would make it automatic.",summary:"Presidential Decree No. 304 of 25 May 2022 extends Russia's simplified citizenship naturalisation procedure (originally applied to occupied Donbas by Decree No. 183 of April 2019) to residents of Kherson Oblast and the parts of Zaporizhzhia Oblast under Russian military control. The decree enables applications to be processed within three months. It was issued approximately three months after Russian forces seized Kherson and northern Zaporizhzhia, and approximately five months before formal annexation (30 September 2022). The decree is step 5 of a 10-step passportisation sequence documented across the citizenship collection. The Ukrainian Ministry of Foreign Affairs described it as a flagrant violation of Ukraine's sovereignty. Ukraine, the EU, the US, and the UN regard Russian citizenship issued to residents of occupied territories as legally null and void.",file:"RF Presidential Decree No. 304, 25 May 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/citizenship/doc-109.html"},
  {id:"DOC-110",date:"Regulatory reference",theme:["Governance","Identity, Culture and Religion"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation: Regulatory Reference — Admission to Citizenship in Simplified Procedure for Foreign Nationals and Stateless Persons",desc:"Official regulatory reference document setting out the simplified citizenship procedure available to foreign nationals and stateless persons, including residents of occupied Ukrainian territories designated by the passportisation decrees. Includes biometric data requirements.",descriptor:"The procedural framework through which Russia administered the passportisation of occupied Ukrainian territories at the individual level: three-month processing and biometric data collection.",summary:"Russian Federation official regulatory reference document governing the simplified citizenship admission procedure for foreign nationals and stateless persons. Sets out eligibility categories (including persons falling within the passportisation decrees of 2019 to 2022), documentation requirements, the three-month processing timeline, and biometric data requirements introduced for applicants from occupied Ukrainian territories. The document is analytically significant as the operational instrument through which the strategic passportisation policy (documented in the presidential decrees) was administered at the individual level. The biometric requirement, fingerprint data and digital photograph, creates a database of identifiers for Ukrainian residents who interact with the system. The treatment of residents who decline Russian citizenship as foreigners eligible for deportation under the April 2023 decree means this procedure functions as both an integration mechanism and a coercion instrument.",file:"RF simplified citizenship procedure regulatory reference",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/citizenship/doc-110.html"},
  {id:"DOC-111",date:"Regulatory reference",theme:["Governance","Identity, Culture and Religion"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation: Regulatory Reference — Admission to Citizenship for Residents of Transnistria (Transnistrian Moldovan Republic)",desc:"Regulatory reference governing Russian citizenship for PMR residents: the earliest and most operationally mature instance of Russia's passportisation model, which was subsequently replicated in South Ossetia, Abkhazia, and occupied Ukrainian territories.",descriptor:"The Transnistrian template for passportisation: the original model Russia refined over twenty years before applying it at scale in occupied Ukraine.",summary:"Russian Federation regulatory reference document governing the simplified citizenship procedure for residents of the Transnistrian Moldovan Republic (PMR), the Russian-backed separatist entity in eastern Moldova. Russia has issued passports to PMR residents since the early 2000s, making Transnistria the earliest operationally mature instance of the passportisation policy. The document is included in the citizenship collection as a comparator: the Transnistrian model (mass issuance of Russian citizenship to residents of an unrecognised entity on a neighbouring state's territory) was subsequently applied in South Ossetia and Abkhazia after 2002 and then, at dramatically greater scale, in occupied Ukrainian territories from 2019. Key differences between the Transnistrian and Ukraine models include: the Ukraine model became collective and coercive after September 2022, whereas the Transnistrian model remained voluntary and individual-application-based.",file:"RF regulatory reference on citizenship for Transnistrian residents",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/citizenship/doc-111.html"},
  {id:"DOC-112",date:"September 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Treaty on the Accession of Kherson Oblast to the Russian Federation (30 September 2022)",desc:"Annexation treaty signed by Putin and occupation head Vladimir Saldo on 30 September 2022, purporting to incorporate Kherson Oblast into the Russian Federation as a new federal subject. Not recognised by Ukraine, the UN, the EU, or the US.",descriptor:"The annexation treaty for Kherson Oblast: the document Russia signed to claim sovereignty over a territory whose administrative centre it had already lost.",summary:"The Treaty on the Accession of Kherson Oblast to the Russian Federation, signed 30 September 2022 at a ceremony in the Grand Kremlin Palace. One of four annexation treaties signed that day for Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson. The treaty follows the Crimea 2014 model, establishing Kherson as a new Russian federal subject, providing for a citizenship transition period, and declaring that existing Ukrainian legal instruments will be superseded by Russian federal law. It invokes September 2022 referendums condemned by international observers as lacking legal validity. Kherson city was recaptured by Ukrainian forces on 11 November 2022, six weeks after signing. The UN General Assembly declared the annexations null and void (Resolution ES-11/4, 143 votes to 5, 12 October 2022). Ratified by federal constitutional law (DOC-113) on 4 October 2022.",file:"Treaty on the Accession of Kherson Oblast to the Russian Federation, 30 September 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/ko-zo-legal-acts/doc-112.html"},
  {id:"DOC-113",date:"October 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation: Federal Constitutional Law on the Ratification of the Treaty on the Accession of Kherson Oblast (4 October 2022)",desc:"Federal constitutional law ratifying the Kherson annexation treaty (DOC-112), adopted unanimously by both chambers of the Federal Assembly on 3 to 4 October 2022 and signed by Putin on 4 October 2022.",descriptor:"The ratification law that caused the Kherson annexation to enter the Russian legal order: its Article 3 declares Kherson's borders to be the state border of the Russian Federation.",summary:"Federal Constitutional Law of the Russian Federation ratifying the Treaty on the Accession of Kherson Oblast (DOC-112), adopted by the State Duma on 3 October 2022 and the Federation Council on 4 October 2022, signed by President Putin on 4 October 2022. The ratification law is the domestic instrument that caused the annexation treaty to enter into force within the Russian legal system, formally establishing Kherson Oblast as a new federal subject, triggering the extension of Russian federal law to the territory, and initiating the citizenship transition period. Article 3 declares that Kherson Oblast's borders constitute the state border of the Russian Federation, providing the legal framework for Russia's characterisation of Ukrainian military operations to retake its own territory as attacks on Russian soil.",file:"RF Federal Constitutional Law on Ratification of Kherson Treaty, 4 October 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/ko-zo-legal-acts/doc-113.html"},
  {id:"DOC-114",date:"October 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation Presidential Decree No. 708 — Appointment of Acting Governor of Kherson Oblast (4 October 2022)",desc:"Presidential Decree No. 708, signed on the same day as the ratification law, appointing Vladimir Saldo as Acting Governor of Kherson Oblast: the immediate step integrating the occupation administration into the Russian federal executive hierarchy.",descriptor:"Decree No. 708: the decree that transformed an occupation administrator into a Russian federal governor on the day annexation entered force.",summary:"Presidential Decree No. 708 of 4 October 2022 appoints Vladimir Saldo as Acting Governor of Kherson Oblast in the Russian Federation system, on the same day the annexation ratification law (DOC-113) entered into force. Saldo had signed the annexation treaty on 30 September 2022 as the representative of Kherson Oblast and was now formally integrated into the Russian federal executive hierarchy. The decree marks the transition from military civil administration to notional civilian federal governance. It is the third step in the Kherson annexation sequence: annexation treaty (30 September, DOC-112), ratification law (4 October, DOC-113), appointment of Acting Governor (4 October, this decree). Saldo is under EU, UK, US, and other sanctions for his role in the occupation and annexation.",file:"RF Presidential Decree No. 708, 4 October 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/ko-zo-legal-acts/doc-114.html"},
  {id:"DOC-115",date:"2024",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Zaporizhzhia Oblast (Occupied): Government Composition Amendment (2024)",desc:"Regulatory act of the occupation administration of Zaporizhzhia Oblast setting out the composition and structure of the oblast government under the Russian federal system, documenting the post-annexation transition from military to notional civilian governance.",descriptor:"Bureaucratised occupation: the Zaporizhzhia occupation government restructured to mirror a standard Russian federal subject, operating from Melitopol while Zaporizhzhia city remains under Ukrainian control.",summary:"Document setting out the composition of the Government of Zaporizhzhia Oblast as a Russian Federation federal subject, issued in 2024. The government structure replicates that of standard Russian federal subjects, with ministries for finance, economy, agriculture, education, health, social protection, transport, and construction. The 2024 composition document reflects the consolidation of the occupation governance structure from a wartime improvisation to a bureaucratised federal body. Zaporizhzhia city, the administrative centre of the oblast, remains under Ukrainian control; the occupation administration operates from Melitopol and Energodar. Names occupation officials in their public governmental capacity; permitted under the research DPO framework.",file:"Zaporizhzhia Oblast government composition document, 2024",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/ko-zo-legal-acts/doc-115.html"},
  {id:"DOC-116",date:"Post-annexation",theme:["Governance","Education and Militarisation"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Zaporizhzhia Oblast (Occupied): Regulations on the Organisation and Activity of the Ministry of Education",desc:"Regulations establishing the Ministry of Education of Zaporizhzhia Oblast under the Russian federal framework: the institutional body through which Ukrainian-language schooling is eliminated and the Russian curriculum imposed.",descriptor:"The legal basis for Russifying Zaporizhzhia's schools: the Ministry created by these regulations is the body mandated to replace Ukrainian education with Russian federal standards.",summary:"Regulations (Polozhenie) governing the organisation and activity of the Ministry of Education of Zaporizhzhia Oblast under the Russian Federation administrative framework, issued after the September 2022 annexation. The regulations establish the Ministry's mandate to implement Russian federal state educational standards, oversee licensing and accreditation of educational organisations, supervise the language of instruction in accordance with Russian federal law (making Russian the state language and eliminating protected status for Ukrainian), and manage teacher training and certification under Russian qualification requirements. The document is the formal legal basis for the institutional elimination of Ukrainian-language schooling in occupied Zaporizhzhia and the imposition of the Russian federal curriculum. Cross-reference: RF Federal Law No. 423-FZ on mandatory military training in schools (DOC-121); DNR Law on Education (DOC-104); Mariupol education programme (DOC-099).",file:"Zaporizhzhia Oblast Ministry of Education regulations, post-annexation",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/ko-zo-legal-acts/doc-116.html"},
  {id:"DOC-117",date:"Pre-February 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Luhansk People's Republic: Law Amending Article 53 of the Law on Military Duty and Military Service",desc:"LNR amendment narrowing the grounds for conscription exemption under Article 53 of the LNR military duty law. The LNR counterpart to the DNR amendment (DOC-107), part of the coordinated pre-invasion tightening of conscription architecture.",descriptor:"The LNR mirror of DNR Law 376-IINs: conscription exemptions narrowed on the Luhansk side in the same pre-invasion legislative sequence.",summary:"Law of the Luhansk People's Republic amending Article 53 of the LNR Law on Military Duty and Military Service, which sets out the grounds on which LNR citizens are not subject to conscription. The amendment narrows these exemption grounds in a manner parallel to the DNR amendment to Article 23 of Law No. 08-INs (DOC-107), adopted in the same period. The synchronisation of LNR and DNR military duty legislative amendments, rather than parallel independent development, reflects Russian oversight of the legislative process in both entities. Read alongside the LNR travel restrictions decree (DOC-118) and the LNR conscription decree of 2025 (DOC-120), this amendment is part of a three-year legislative sequence governing compulsory military service in LNR-controlled territory.",file:"LNR Law amending Article 53, Law on Military Duty",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/lnr-legal-acts/doc-117.html"},
  {id:"DOC-118",date:"February 2022",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Luhansk People's Republic: Decree on Restrictions on Departure of Citizens Subject to Military Duty During General Mobilisation",desc:"LNR decree prohibiting conscription-age men from leaving LNR-controlled territory without military commissariat permission during the period of general mobilisation declared February 2022. The enforcement corollary of the mobilisation order.",descriptor:"The travel ban that closed the exit route: LNR men of fighting age could not leave, and those who remained were subject to forced enlistment.",summary:"Decree of the Head of the Luhansk People's Republic imposing restrictions on the departure of male citizens subject to military duty from LNR-controlled territory during the period of general mobilisation declared in February 2022. The decree is the enforcement corollary of the general mobilisation declaration: it closes the avenue of evasion by departure, requiring military commissariat authorisation for any permitted exceptions (medical treatment, family emergency, other exceptional circumstances). The combination of a general mobilisation declaration, a travel restriction preventing departure, and the obligation to report to commissariats creates a coercive conscription environment. Under international humanitarian law, the prohibition on an occupying power compelling civilians of the occupied territory to serve in its armed forces (Hague Regulations, Article 52; Fourth Geneva Convention, Article 51) applies to this context.",file:"LNR Decree on travel restrictions during general mobilisation, February 2022",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/lnr-legal-acts/doc-118.html"},
  {id:"DOC-119",date:"2025",theme:["Governance","Education and Militarisation"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Presidential Instructions: Expansion of the Defenders of the Fatherland Foundation to Occupied Ukrainian Oblasts (2025)",desc:"Kremlin instructions directing the extension of the Defenders of the Fatherland Foundation to the four occupied Ukrainian oblasts: a state welfare and ideological integration instrument tied to military mobilisation.",descriptor:"The veterans' foundation that doubles as an ideological tool: extended to occupied Ukraine in 2025 to run patriotic school programmes and normalise military service.",summary:"Presidential instructions (poruchieniia) issued in 2025 directing the extension of the Defenders of the Fatherland Foundation to the four occupied Ukrainian oblasts annexed by Russia in September 2022. The Foundation, established by Presidential Decree in April 2023, provides personalised social support, rehabilitation, and psychological assistance to veterans of the war and their families. Its extension to the occupied territories serves multiple functions: social welfare infrastructure for the growing veteran population; an additional institutional presence of the Russian federal government; and loyalty and dependency relationships with military families that function as a social stabilisation mechanism. The Foundation's school-based programmes honour war participants and contribute to patriotic education curricula, making it an instrument of the education militarisation documented across the TOT Insights collection.",file:"Kremlin presidential instructions on Defenders of the Fatherland Foundation, 2025",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/lnr-legal-acts/doc-119.html"},
  {id:"DOC-120",date:"March 2025",theme:["Governance","Accountability and Legal"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Luhansk Oblast (Occupied): Conscription Decree No. UG-341/25 — Spring 2025 Call-Up (28 March 2025)",desc:"Decree No. UG-341/25 of 28 March 2025 initiating the spring 2025 conscription call-up in Russian-controlled Luhansk Oblast, targeting citizens born between 1995 and 2007 (the youngest cohort aged 17 to 18).",descriptor:"Luhansk spring 2025 call-up: the 2007 birth cohort (aged 17 to 18) conscripted into the war their occupation-era schooling prepared them for.",summary:"Decree No. UG-341/25 of the head of the occupation administration of Luhansk Oblast, signed 28 March 2025, initiating the spring 2025 conscription call-up for male citizens born between 1995 and 2007. The youngest cohort (born 2007) are aged 17 to 18 at the time of the decree. The targeting of persons born in 2007, who were aged approximately 14 to 15 at the full-scale invasion and who have grown up under Russian occupation and education, documents the conscription of the first generation schooled entirely under the Russian curriculum and mandatory patriotic education programmes. The decree also reveals that conscription continues under occupation-administration instruments three years after formal annexation, rather than being fully transferred to Russian federal procedures. The inclusion of a cohort including persons aged 17 raises questions under the Optional Protocol to the CRC on children in armed conflict, to which Russia is a party.",file:"LNR Decree No. UG-341/25, 28 March 2025",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/lnr-legal-acts/doc-120.html"},
  {id:"DOC-121",date:"August 2023",theme:["Governance","Education and Militarisation","Coercion, Resistance and Security"],format:"Primary Sources",title:"Russian Federation Federal Law No. 423-FZ — Mandatory Military Training in Schools (4 August 2023)",desc:"Federal Law No. 423-FZ introduces mandatory military basic training as a compulsory secondary school subject from September 2024. Because the occupied Ukrainian oblasts are formally annexed, the law applies in Russian-controlled Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson.",descriptor:"The law that put weapons handling, drone operation, and combat first aid into Russian school curricula (and, via annexation, into occupied Ukrainian schools) from September 2024.",summary:"Federal Law No. 423-FZ of 4 August 2023 amends the Federal Law on Education in the Russian Federation to introduce Foundations of Security and Defence of the Motherland (OBZR) as a mandatory subject in years 10 and 11 of the school curriculum from 1 September 2024. The subject replaces the previous Fundamentals of Life Safety (OBZh) with significantly expanded military content: weapons handling, tactical medicine (combat first aid), drone operation, civil defence, and the legal basis of Russia's armed conflict. Because the four occupied Ukrainian oblasts are formally incorporated into the Russian Federation, the law applies in schools in Russian-controlled Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson operating under Russian federal educational standards. The law is the most direct legislative expression of education militarisation documented across the TOT Insights collection. Cross-reference: DNR Mobilisation Law (DOC-106); ZO Ministry of Education Regulations (DOC-116); DNR Law on Education (DOC-104); LNR Conscription Decree (DOC-120).",file:"RF Federal Law No. 423-FZ, 4 August 2023",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"primary-sources/mobilisation-martial-law/doc-121.html"},
  {id:"DOC-L1",date:"April 2026",theme:["Economics","Governance"],format:"Visual",title:"Russian Developer Networks in Occupied Ukraine: Corporate Ownership Map",desc:"Corporate ownership map tracing construction companies in occupied Donetsk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson to their parent structures. Four ownership clusters; nine companies linked to registry records. The Etalon Group cluster - a major listed St Petersburg developer with an occupied-territory subsidiary structure routed through AO RSK and OOO SFO Direct Finance - is the analytically significant structural finding.",descriptor:"Four ownership clusters, one major listed St Petersburg developer with an occupied-territory subsidiary structure - corporate ownership map of Russian construction networks in occupied Donetsk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson.",summary:"TOT Insights corporate ownership investigation tracing construction and developer companies operating in occupied Donetsk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson to their parent corporate structures and holding entities. Four ownership clusters identified; multiple nominally independent companies share common ownership structures and geographic origins per registry records. Most significant structural finding: Etalon Group (INN 7806001950), a major listed St Petersburg developer, has an occupied-territory subsidiary structure routed through AO RSK and OOO SFO Direct Finance (closed fund ownership). Nine companies linked to registry records; company nodes link to EGRUL entries. Source: TOT Insights OSINT investigation, 2025-2026.",file:"TOT Insights OSINT investigation, 2025–2026",gdocs:"",dashboardFile:"dashboards/developer_network_visualisations.html"}
];

